  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical findings and facility classifications are demonstrated using a single case study of Spokane County.",
      "what": "The study introduces a novel fairness metric combined with network centrality to evaluate facility placement, applying it to census tracts in Spokane County, Washington. Using cluster analysis on public demographic and socioeconomic data, the researchers assessed the distribution of existing facilities. The analysis revealed that while undesirable facilities are inequitably distributed, key desirable facilities like hospitals and parks are optimally located for both fairness and centrality.",
      "why": "It provides a replicable framework for planners to balance spatial efficiency with demographic equity, moving beyond pure centrality optimization which often exacerbates disparate access to services."
    }
